      "body": "The notebook loads one day of SPX option trades (2026-09-08), backs out the risk-free and dividend rates from put-call parity, builds a Treasury zero curve, computes implied vols, and plots the vol surface. It has 21 cells, all code; the \"headings\" are `###` comments.\n\n## What each part does\n\n| Cells | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| 0\u20132 | A note that \"the time stamp for the MassiveHistorical data is not quite right\", then a path setup and the download step: equity bars, rates and dividends for SPY/SPX, 2026-09-08 to 09-11. |\n| 3\u20135 | Loads SPXW and SPX bars for 2026-09-08 into `market`. Makes `df_calibration`: SPXW bars between 15:30 and 16:00 ET. |\n| 6 | `build_parity_pairs`: finds strikes where a call and a put traded in the same minute, keeps strikes within 10% of spot, and flags box-spread prints. Result: 13,907 pairs, 68 box prints, 22 expiries. |\n| 7 | `regress_parity`: for each expiry, regresses call minus put on strike, with a separate intercept for each minute. This gives the discount factor D, a dividend discount, implied r and q, and standard errors. |\n| 8 | Two charts: when the matched pairs traded and how many each expiry has, plus one regression panel per expiry. |\n| 9\u201312 | Builds the Treasury zero curve from par yields (3.81%\u20135.25%) and plots its par, zero and forward rates. |\n| 13\u201315 | `contract_implied_vols`: works out time to settlement (SPXW settles at the 16:00 close, SPX monthlies at the open), the curve rate r(T), the forward using a flat 1.1% dividend yield, and Black-76 implied vols. `contract_vol_summary` averages each contract by volume and marks which side is out of the money. 13,291 of 13,548 bars got an implied vol; 2,975 contracts, 2,439 out of the money. |\n| 16\u201317 | `plot_vol_surface`: smiles for each expiry plus the at-the-money term structure. `plot_vol_surface_3d` is defined but never run. |\n| 18\u201320 | Unfinished: a comment cut off mid-sentence (\"filtering out the underlying ticker is\"), a stray `1`, and an empty cell. |\n\n## Problems I noticed\n\n**It won't run from a fresh kernel.** The cells were run out of order, and some depend on things that only exist in the live session:\n- Cell 9 calls `treasury_zero_curve` before cell 15 imports it.\n- Cell 10 uses `treasury_curve`, which isn't created until cell 15. It also stores forward rates in a variable named `zero`.\n- Cell 12 plots `discount_curve`, which isn't defined anywhere in the notebook.\n\n**Things that may relate to the \"surface not quite right\" issue:**\n- The forward uses a fixed 1.1% dividend yield and the Treasury curve. The r and q the notebook estimates from parity in cell 7 are never used. SPX options are usually priced off rates nearer box-spread levels, which run above T-bills, so using Treasury rates could skew the forwards and the smiles.\n- Cell 5's comment says 16:00 is excluded, but the code uses `<=`, so a 16:00 bar would be kept.\n- Some parity fits look noisy: the 09-10 expiry has D = 1.0005, which implies a negative rate.\n- The note in cell 0 about wrong timestamps matters here: T is measured in seconds to the close, and the shortest expiries are only minutes away.\n\n**Minor:** cell 2 imports `sys`, `Path` and `Timestamp` without using them, and the colour constants are copied into three cells.\n\nI can turn this into a notebook that runs top to bottom, or use the parity-implied rates for the forwards and see whether the smiles look better.",
